{:edb/db-before-t 1968 :edb/db-after-t 1969 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535281 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:10:23.486Z" 13194139535281 true] [17592186341337 1100 "Let $F(x,y)=\\langle x^3+2y^2,x^2+\\ln y\\rangle$ for $y>0$, and let $D$ be the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(2,0)$ and $(1,1)$. For $0<\\epsilon<1$, let $C_\\epsilon$ be the positively oriented (counterclockwise) boundary of $D_\\epsilon=D\\cap\\{y\\ge\\epsilon\\}$. Use Green's theorem on $D_\\epsilon$ to evaluate the limiting circulation\n\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{lim}}=\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\oint_{C_\\epsilon}F\\cdot dr.$$\n\nThis explicit one-sided limit is the intended interpretation. The original ordinary line integral over the untruncated triangle is undefined because $\\ln y$ is undefined on its entire bottom edge." 13194139535281 true] [17592186341337 1100 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{3}+2{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,0)$ and $(1,1).$" 13194139535281 false] [17592186341337 1145 "For each $0<\\epsilon<1$, the field is continuously differentiable on an open neighborhood of $D_\\epsilon$, since that region stays in $y>0$. Its positively oriented boundary has vertices $(\\epsilon,\\epsilon)$, $(2-\\epsilon,\\epsilon)$ and $(1,1)$. Green's theorem therefore applies there.\n\nWith $P=x^3+2y^2$ and $Q=x^2+\\ln y$,\n\n$$Q_x-P_y=2x-4y.$$\n\nThe truncated triangle is described by $\\epsilon\\le y\\le1$ and $y\\le x\\le2-y$. Hence\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{C_\\epsilon}F\\cdot dr&=\\int_\\epsilon^1\\int_y^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,dx\\,dy\\\\&=\\int_\\epsilon^1(4-12y+8y^2)\\,dy\\\\&=\\frac23-4\\epsilon+6\\epsilon^2-\\frac83\\epsilon^3.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTaking $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ gives $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{lim}}=\\frac23$, the unchanged displayed option b.\n\nThe boundary interpretation also converges directly. The $\\ln y\\,dy$ term has primitive $H(y)=y\\ln y-y$, and $H(y)\\to0$ as $y\\downarrow0$. Each sloping side thus has its own finite one-sided integral; no cancellation of divergent integrals or principal-value convention is needed. On each truncated horizontal bottom edge, $dy=0$, while $F$ is defined because $y=\\epsilon>0$. The polynomial terms also converge. These facts establish the specified limiting circulation without assigning a value to $F$ on the original edge $y=0$ or claiming ordinary Green's theorem applies to the untruncated boundary." 13194139535281 true] [17592186341337 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/73bf9b973e50c540a873f4f1a44d936474e4c49d7041a330902508a840645009.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):y\\le x\\le 2-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}-4xy]}_{y}^{2-y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-y)^{2}-4(2-y)y-{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{2}-12y+4)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-3y+1)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535281 false]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
